WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.397
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:15.820
Unit eighteen: Mediation Techniques. Today you will contrast approaches with rather than + noun / gerund.

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.656
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.879
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:24.671
Mediator.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.424
Mediator means a neutral person who helps parties reach agreement.

8
00:00:30.017 --> 00:00:32.991
The mediator met each family separately first.

9
00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.340
Your turn. Say: Mediator.

10
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.097
Mediator.

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:41.956
Neutrality.

12
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.389
Neutrality means not supporting either side in a dispute.

13
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:50.631
Neutrality requires the mediator to avoid personal opinions.

14
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.962
Your turn. Say: Neutrality.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.987
Neutrality.

16
00:00:58.750 --> 00:00:59.972
Facilitation.

17
00:01:00.983 --> 00:01:04.887
Facilitation means making a discussion easier and more productive.

18
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:08.435
Skilled facilitation kept the meeting focused.

19
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.551
Your turn. Say: Facilitation.

20
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.738
Facilitation.

21
00:01:16.483 --> 00:01:18.042
Interest-based approach.

22
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:24.184
Interest-based approach means focusing on underlying needs rather than stated demands.

23
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:29.082
An interest-based approach revealed that both sides wanted security.

24
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:33.462
Your turn. Say: Interest-based approach.

25
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.926
Interest-based approach.

26
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:40.650
Reframing.

27
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:46.111
Reframing means restating a hostile comment in neutral, constructive terms.

28
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:50.063
Reframing turned an accusation into a shared problem.

29
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.256
Your turn. Say: Reframing.

30
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.206
Reframing.

31
00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:58.742
Trust.

32
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.387
Trust means confidence in someone's honesty and fairness.

33
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:07.446
Without trust in the mediator, the process collapses.

34
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.540
Your turn. Say: Trust.

35
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.157
Trust.

36
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:16.208
Impartiality.

37
00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:21.678
Impartiality means treating all parties equally and without bias.

38
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:25.212
Impartiality must be visible as well as real.

39
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:28.957
Your turn. Say: Impartiality.

40
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.126
Impartiality.

41
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:35.047
Resolution.

42
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.386
Resolution means the settlement of a dispute.

43
00:02:38.983 --> 00:02:41.610
The resolution was signed in the village pagoda.

44
00:02:42.217 --> 00:02:44.962
Your turn. Say: Resolution.

45
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.987
Resolution.

46
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.277
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:54.117 --> 00:03:00.484
Visal, a landlord says, 'The tenant is lazy and never pays on time.' How should a mediator respond?

48
00:03:01.583 --> 00:03:09.530
By reframing rather than repeating the insult: 'It sounds as though reliable payment is very important to you.'

49
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:12.291
Isn't that avoiding the issue?

50
00:03:13.383 --> 00:03:21.704
Not at all. It moves the discussion towards needs rather than blame. Mediators focus on interests rather than fixed positions.

51
00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:24.630
And if the tenant gets angry?

52
00:03:25.717 --> 00:03:35.541
The mediator acknowledges the emotion rather than suppressing it, then asks what would make payment easier, perhaps a different due date.

53
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.049
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.883 --> 00:03:41.893
Grammar focus: Rather than plus noun and gerund.

55
00:03:42.193 --> 00:03:43.484
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.183 --> 00:03:47.576
Mediators focus on interests rather than fixed positions.

57
00:03:48.483 --> 00:03:51.563
The mediator reframed the insult rather than repeating it.

58
00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.738
Rather than imposing a solution, she asked questions.

59
00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:09.186
'Rather than' links parallel items: nouns (interests rather than positions) or verbs. At the start of a sentence, use -ing: Rather than imposing… After 'would rather', use the base verb: I'd rather listen than argue.

60
00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:14.170
Be careful! Don't say: Focus on interests rather than to fight.

61
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:18.911
Say: Focus on interests rather than fighting.

62
00:04:19.211 --> 00:04:20.688
Rather than + -ing.

63
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.199
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.477
The Mediator's Toolkit.

65
00:04:26.777 --> 00:04:32.411
Mediation differs from arbitration in that the mediator facilitates agreement rather than imposing a decision.

66
00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:41.228
Impartiality is the mediator's most valuable asset, since parties will reveal their true concerns only to someone they trust to be fair.

67
00:04:42.233 --> 00:04:49.423
Reframing transforms accusations into statements of need, allowing discussion to focus on solutions rather than blame.

68
00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:58.366
Private sessions, sometimes called caucuses, allow each party to express emotions and test proposals without losing face.

69
00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:06.660
In Cambodia, traditional mediation by village elders and monks shares many of these principles with modern professional practice.

70
00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:11.919
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:12.783 --> 00:05:15.503
She chose to walk rather than blank the bus.

72
00:05:15.803 --> 00:05:19.003
A, taking. B, take. C, took.

73
00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:24.334
The answer is B: take.

74
00:05:25.483 --> 00:05:28.481
Treating all parties equally and without bias is blank.

75
00:05:28.781 --> 00:05:33.375
A, impartiality. B, facilitation. C, reframing.

76
00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:39.293
The answer is A: impartiality.

77
00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:43.750
Restating a hostile comment in neutral terms is blank.

78
00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:48.307
A, resolution. B, neutrality. C, reframing.

79
00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:53.913
The answer is C: reframing.

80
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.748
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:57.048 --> 00:06:03.128
A, Rather than argue, they listened.. B, Rather than arguing, they listened.. C, Rather arguing, they listened..

82
00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:09.607
The answer is B: Rather than arguing, they listened..

83
00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.955
According to the reading, mediation differs from arbitration because the mediator…

84
00:06:15.255 --> 00:06:20.256
A, imposes a decision. B, facilitates agreement. C, charges more.

85
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.705
The answer is B: facilitates agreement.

86
00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.924
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:34.665
Why is impartiality so important for a mediator?

88
00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:52.857
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:53.157 --> 00:07:28.416
Impartiality is essential because mediation depends entirely on both parties' willingness to speak openly. If one side suspects the mediator favours the other, it will hide its real concerns, and any agreement will feel imposed rather than chosen. A mediator must therefore manage not only actual bias but also perceptions, for example by giving each side equal time and avoiding personal opinions. In my view, impartiality is what gives a mediator authority without power.

90
00:07:28.716 --> 00:07:30.251
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.474
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and rather than plus noun and gerund.

92
00:07:35.774 --> 00:07:40.563
Today's value is impartiality. Treat every side fairly, without taking sides.

93
00:07:40.863 --> 00:07:43.538
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:43.838 --> 00:07:46.734
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

